title The commit no hook saw -- REDS %338 booked, repaired, and walled
prompt Open as HUSH under the INNER LOOP Standing block -- roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, QA on touch, the council rota, prose witnesses on prose sends, stamp-and-name, ASCII-first, READ SCOPE -- pick up the running thread and take the Pond lane under the accrete-only gate boundary.
think ListAgents named no reachable peer, so nothing brushed and nothing needed a nod before a file moved. The tree opened clean at 12d675d65e with two stashes from dead laps, both reported by the runner and neither mine to spend.
think The council rota read row 1, Air and Saturn -- law and boundary, the sense touch. The last recorded row was 4 at N=3,684; N is git rev-list --count HEAD at 3,701 and 3,701 mod 5 is 1, so the rota advanced rather than repeated and no hand advance was owed. Its threshold page asks the lap to run a hand along every fence and press: if the hand passes through, the boundary was a wish. Its fixed seat, Single-Stranded, says you check whether a part stands free by trying to pull it out. Both turned out to be the lap.
think The roster went red at geode_libraries and then at readme_metrics -- both generated front-door pages, stale, on a clean tree behind a green round. Reds first, so the Pond work stopped there.
think The mechanism, found by reading both halves of one seam. Commit 12d675d65e added tools/p/pond_enclosure_built_witness.rish. That is exactly rule one of tools/hooks/pre-commit -- a witness arriving moves the witness counts on README.md and docs-geode/libraries/README.md, so the hook renders both and stages them into that same commit. It rendered neither, because that round was re-derived onto a rewritten upstream and its work replayed by git cherry-pick, and git runs pre-commit for git commit and git commit --amend and for no other path.
think I proved it rather than recalling it, on real repositories in a throwaway pen: git commit fires pre-commit and post-commit; --amend fires both; cherry-pick and rebase fire post-commit alone; merge --no-ff fires neither. Nothing in this tree had that table written down, and the tree's own twice-pulled send REQUIRES a rebase whenever the anointed remote moved -- so the skipping paths are the ordinary close of a contested round.
think The design choice worth naming. The obvious reading detects the sequencer by probing .git/rebase-merge, .git/rebase-apply, .git/sequencer, CHERRY_PICK_HEAD -- an inference from four situations to the property actually wanted. tools/hooks/post-commit asks the property directly: did my sibling run? pre-commit drops a token on its success path and post-commit reads it, so a path git adds tomorrow answers right on its first run without anyone editing a list of four names. Single-stranded: one question, no roster to keep current.
think Both markers live inside the git directory, never in the work tree. That is the safety property rather than a tidiness one -- a rebase refuses to continue over unstaged changes, so a hook writing into the tree mid-replay could wedge the very rebase it was helping.
think Rendering in post-commit was weighed and declined on measurement: the two generators take 3.1s and 9.4s on this bench, so a rebase replaying ten commits would spend two minutes answering a question the next commit answers free. The debt is recorded there and paid by the next pre-commit, which already knows how to refresh a page and how to behave about an author's unstaged edits.
think Where the marker is cleared decided one real case. pre-commit only READS it; post-commit clears it when a commit actually LANDS. Clearing it in pre-commit would let a refusal further down forget a debt still owed, and the control proves that leg from the refusing side.
obs The control grew 8 proven behaviors to 14, every one on real git repositories in a pen: the debt recorded on a cherry-pick and on a rebase, the debt paid by a commit adding no witness of its own, rule one still RESTING when no debt stands (so the second trigger did not turn it into fire-on-everything), the debt KEPT across a refusal, and post-commit resting with no rishi on disk exactly as pre-commit does. control_verdict=ok, and the witness GREEN on metal.
obs The control caught a behavior nobody asked for, and it is worth keeping. Its own seed commit uses --no-verify, which skips pre-commit exactly as a cherry-pick does -- so post-commit recorded a debt for it and case 1 read that debt rather than its own trigger. The fixture clears the marker after planting, and the property stands: a commit reaching for the flag this tree forbids leaves a mark.
obs What this does not reach, said where it binds rather than implied. git merge --no-ff creates a commit and runs neither hook. This tree's seated send merges --ff-only, which creates no commit and stales nothing, so the gap sits off the path the tree walks. A post-merge hook was declined rather than forgotten: it fires on every git pull fast-forward too and would mark a debt no local commit incurred.
obs My own deviation, owned. The card says open the roster cold and HOLD STILL while it runs. I opened it and began editing inside the same minute, so that cold pass measures two trees and its verdict is tree_moved by my own hand -- the runner is right and I was wrong. The reading that counts for this round is the --hot pass after git add, run while holding still, and it is the one this log reports.
obs The Pond lane's own measurement rode along and is the next lap's warm start. Under the launcher's exact flags (--private-home --no-docker --no-gpu) the realized mount table inside a running enclosure agrees with the plan on the home: three rows, matching. Under NO flags the default passes nine host dotdirs through -- .config, .cache, .codex, .claude, .local, .cursor, .gitconfig, .claude.json read-write or read-only, plus .nix-profile -- and adds /dev/dri. So --private-home means dotdirs are NOT passed through, which the help text leaves ambiguous and the measurement settles. Left as a reading rather than a record change: the record describes what agent-jail.sh builds, and it builds with the flags.
obs The realized table also carries three kernel interfaces under the whole-/sys map that no mask closes -- /sys/fs/bpf, /sys/kernel/tracing, /sys/kernel/config -- beside the four the record names. Naming them widens what the record admits and loosens nothing, yet it is orbit two's own material and orbit two brushes Dream's lane, so it is surfaced here rather than written by this lap.
obs The send met the collision this tree already has a law for, and the law worked. xy had moved six commits, and one of them booked %337 too -- theirs stamped 20260829.013811, mine 20260829.013539, so MINE IS THE EARLIER STAMP AND STILL THE ONE THAT MOVED. The derived-spine law says publication decides rather than stamp order: their number reached the anointed spine and froze, mine was local and unshared, so it renumbers to %338 and keeps its stamp as its key. Recorded in the row itself rather than smoothed over, since a law is worth more when a reader can see it bite.
obs And the rebase proved this round's own repair by doing it. Replaying my commit onto xy skipped pre-commit exactly as the red describes, tools/hooks/post-commit printed the debt line and wrote derived-pages-owed, and the continue commit's own pre-commit read that marker and refreshed both pages. The fix was field-tested by the very send that carried it.
obs An orphan found and reaped, recorded rather than booked. A `standing_equipment_run.sh --hot` process from the previous lap had been running 31 minutes unread -- started 01:13:00, the same second commit 12d675d65e was authored -- so that round launched its hot verification and its session ended before the pass reported. Two roster runs were contending for this laptop, which is why the cold pass crawled. I killed the orphan: its reading measures a tree that has since moved and can no longer be honest. Not booked as a red, since a session ending is a runtime fact rather than a tree fault and that lap's own witnesses were run individually GREEN -- yet it is the same family as %223 one step later: a lap ends at the commit AND at the report of the pass that verifies it.
obs A second, smaller sync repair rode along in my own lane. pond/enclosure_policy.rye documented max_declarations as "four times the largest real policy in this tree: the agent-jail lap declares eleven". That record grew from six mounts to thirty-two declarations on 20260828, so the bound is twice rather than four times, and the comment now carries the measured count with its date and the way the parser counts it. A bound's reason is worth no more than its number.
